# {{ cluster_name }} development

Infrastructure as code for one Proxmox + ZFS + k3s cluster, generated from
weisssrv-cluster-template. This skill is the operating map: it encodes the
workflow and the invariants, and points at the documents that hold everything
else. It does not restate them — open the file it names. Paths are
repository-relative.

`README.md` is the top-level fact source, `docs/` holds this cluster's
procedures, and `task --list` owns the command set.

## Invariants

Violating one of these breaks production or fails review.

- **Never push to the default branch.** Every change ships as a branch plus a
  merge request, including one-line fixes. Flux and the deploy jobs act on the
  default branch, so a direct push is an unreviewed deploy.
- **No secrets in git.** Host tooling references
  {% if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}`op://{{ onepassword_vault }}/<item>/<field>`{% else %}the secrets backend{% endif %};
  workloads read a Secret produced by an `ExternalSecret`. A new credential is
  added to the vault and documented, never inlined.
- **Flux owns all of `kubernetes/`.** No `kubectl apply -k` and no `helm upgrade`
  as ongoing operations. The only exceptions: `task flux:dev-apply -- <path>`
  (reverted on the next reconcile), the two bootstrap Secrets, and objects
  Ansible provisions outside `kubernetes/`.
- **Kubernetes manifests carry no site literals.** Domains, addresses and CIDRs
  come from the `cluster-config` ConfigMap as `${cluster_...}` substitutions;
  versions come from `cluster-versions`. Writing `{{ internal_domain }}` or an
  address into a manifest is a review rejection — it is the exact debt this
  repository was generated to avoid.
- **Versions are single-sourced** in `ansible/inventories/prod/group_vars/all.yml`.
  After changing a pin, run `task flux:sync-versions` and commit the regenerated
  ConfigMap in the same commit. Never hand-edit a generated file.
- **Ansible roles are not vendored.** There is no `ansible/roles/`. Playbooks
  address `weisssrv.infra.<role>` by FQCN at the ref pinned in
  `ansible/requirements.yml`. A role needing a change is changed in the library
  and pulled in by bumping that ref — not copied in here.
- **Never automate ZFS pool creation or destruction.** Ansible sets properties,
  creates datasets and zvols, and mounts them. Pools are manual.
- **Every service is observed.** A change that adds a service is incomplete
  without logs, metrics, a down-or-stale alert, and a probe if users reach it
  directly.
- **No AI attribution** anywhere: commits, code, comments, documentation, merge
  request text.

## Decision tree

| Changing | Read first | Then |
|---|---|---|
| A Kubernetes application | the closest existing directory under `kubernetes/apps/` | mirror its shape: namespace, workload, storage, ExternalSecret, certificate, ingress route, NetworkPolicy, autoscaling policy |
| A host or guest | `ansible/inventories/prod/` and the role's README in the library | inventory first, playbook second; per-host facts go in `host_vars/` |
| Terraform state (DNS, tailnet, SSO) | the module directory and its README | plan, review, apply; SSO objects are a supervised apply |
| A version pin or an upgrade | `docs/` § upgrades | `task maintenance:check-versions`, then bump, sync, commit both files |
| An incident | the service's dashboard, then its logs | `task flux:status`, `task infra:verify`, `task k3s:status` |
| Cluster access or a credential | `docs/` § secrets | `task flux:rotate-secret`, or re-run the playbook for host-side values |

## Pre-merge gates

Run from the repository root:

- `task lint` — the local mirror of the CI lint stage. Run it for every change.
- `task flux:lint` — whenever `kubernetes/` changed. It renders every
  Kustomization, substitutes, and fails on any unresolved `${...}`.
- `task flux:sync-versions` — whenever a version pin changed; commit both files.
- `task hosts:sync` — whenever `hosts.yml` changed; commit the generated file.
- `task lint:prometheus-config` — whenever alert or recording rules changed. It
  is not part of `task lint`.
- `task infra:check -- --limit <host>` — dry-run before deploying an Ansible
  change to a live host.

## Review and merge

- Merge request pipelines run the path-filtered lint, validate, test and
  security gates. They do not deploy.
- Resolve every valid review finding and re-run the gates before merging. Fold
  any available version bumps into the same revision rather than opening a
  separate one.
- Merge request descriptions state what was tested, in the past tense — not a
  checklist of intentions.
- After merge: Flux reconciles `kubernetes/` within about a minute
  (`task flux:reconcile` forces it) and the deploy jobs run the Ansible side.
  Verify with `task flux:status`, `task infra:verify`, `task k3s:status`.

## Upstream

The platform this cluster is assembled from lives in **weisssrv-lib**: the
Ansible collection, the CI templates, the Terraform modules and the helper
scripts, all pinned at one ref. The generator is
**weisssrv-cluster-template**; `.copier-answers.yml` records what this cluster
was generated from, and `copier update` pulls in template improvements. A fix
that would benefit any cluster belongs upstream, not here.
